{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to [[speed up]].
#: ''He '''gunned''' the engine.''
# {{lb|en|informal}} To offer vigorous support to (a person or cause).
#: ''We're all '''gunning''' for you.''
# {{lb|en|informal}} (gunning for something or gunning to do something) make a great effort.
#* {{quote-web|en|year=2023|author=George Ramsay; Amy Woodyatt|title=‘Like a chairlift up Everest’: Once running’s supreme challenge, has the value of a four-minute mile diminished?|passage=Australian John Landy, one of Bannister’s rivals also gunning to break the four-minute barrier, took more than a second off the Briton’s time in Turku, Finland, a few weeks later.|publisher=CNN|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2023/05/06/sport/running-four-minute-mile-spt-intl/index.html}}
# To seek to attack someone; to take [[aim]] at someone; used with {{m|en|for}}.
#: ''He's been '''gunning''' for you ever since you embarrassed him at the party.''
# To practice [[fowl]]ing or hunting small game; chiefly in participial form: ''to go gunning''.<!--Webster 1913-->
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|prison slang}} To [[masturbate]] while observing and visible to a [[corrections officer]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Jens Soering|title=Yoga of Heart|passage=In the cell diagonally across from mine, a prisoner regularly “'''gunned''' down” the nurse on her morning rounds. He deliberately masturbated so that she'd see him through the cell door window as he ejaculated.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2010|title=BNA's Employment Discrimination Report
|passage={{...}} all inmates participated in such conduct, and {{...}} "the inmates '''gunned''' only female staff, not the all-male security staff," he said.}}

